description:
Location: src/filenode.rs:54-86 (handle_filenode_get, fetchParents branch).

The current shape:

    if let Some(Value::Array(list)) = response.get("list") {      // immutable borrow of response
        let existing_ids: HashSet<String> = list.iter()
            .filter_map(|item| item.get("id")...)
            .collect();
        let node_ids: Vec<Id> = existing_ids.iter()...collect();
        if !node_ids.is_empty() {
            if let Ok(ancestors) = backend.get_ancestors(...).await {
                let list = response["list"].as_array_mut().expect("list must be array");
                //                                                ^^^^^^ workaround
                for ancestor in ancestors {
                    ...
                    list.push(v);
                }
            }
        }
    }

The .expect() is the symptom. The author needed an immutable borrow first (to read existing_ids) then a mutable borrow later (to push ancestors). The immutable borrow lives across an .await, which forces it to be dropped before the mutable borrow can begin. Re-acquiring via response["list"].as_array_mut() then requires an .expect() because the compiler cannot prove the shape is still Array (even though we just destructured Value::Array four lines earlier).

What idiomatic Rust would do: use let-else (Rust 1.65+) to bind the immutable shape, collect what is needed, drop the borrow before .await, then take a single mutable borrow at the end:

    let Some(list_ref) = response.get("list").and_then(Value::as_array) else {
        return Ok((response, tail));
    };
    let existing_ids: HashSet<String> = list_ref.iter().filter_map(...).collect();
    let node_ids: Vec<Id> = existing_ids.iter().map(|s| Id::from(s.as_str())).collect();
    drop(list_ref); // explicit, though NLL handles it
    if node_ids.is_empty() {
        return Ok((response, tail));
    }
    let Ok(ancestors) = backend.get_ancestors(caller, &account_id, &node_ids).await else {
        return Ok((response, tail));
    };
    let Some(list_mut) = response.get_mut("list").and_then(Value::as_array_mut) else {
        // Should be unreachable — we just verified shape — but recoverable instead of panic
        return Ok((response, tail));
    };
    for ancestor in ancestors { ... }

Why it matters: the current .expect() is provably unreachable today, but it is the kind of unreachable-by-construction that breaks under refactor. A future change that moves the .await above the existing_ids computation, or that swaps response between branches, can silently turn this into a real panic. The let-else form removes the panic and documents the shape requirement at the point of use.

Production-code-panic guidance: per the supplement, .expect() in production code needs a comment explaining why it cannot fail. There is no such comment here. The implicit reasoning ('we just matched Value::Array') lives in the reader's head, not in the code.

Canonical comparison: jmap-mail-server has zero .expect() calls in its handlers outside the derive(Serialize)-infallible idiom. The fetchParents pattern is filenode-specific (no analog in mail-server).
